e argument that Japan no  longer has naval superiority in the  Pacific it doesn't have the Americans  don't have superiority themselves it's  more like parody but in the long term  you can see that the trends will not be  good for the Japanese because we're  training more planes we're going to  eventually send over a hundred thousand  planes think of it a hundred thousand  planes to the Pacific to be based at  bases and on carriers the Japanese in  this timeframe will build about ten  thousand for carrier and land-based  operations and so in 1942 there you're  starting to see a change and then in  five key naval battles in succession in  the fall of 1943 off Guadalcanal in 243  the Americans will essentially even  though they will lose two more carriers  and with the Lexington gone and the  Yorktown gone and the Hornet will be  sunk and the wasp will be sunk four and  the Saratoga will be torpedoed for a  brief moment brief moment the Japanese  are excited that only the enterprise  seems to be out there and it's wounded  what they don't quite understand is as I  said there's 27 Essex carriers are now  going to start to appear but income  recompense for that they have lost a  number of cruisers they have lost a  light carrier they have lost a number of  destroy  and they've lost Guadalcanal as 1943  starts the American submarine fleet  fixes its torpedo problems it gets new  classes of submarines it's allowed to  venture further and further from Pearl  Harbor and you're going to start to see  the effect of these tactics these new  ships and especially new Hellcat  fighters Avengers and Helldiver dive  bombers and in a series of battles in 44  off the Mariana Islands we notice a  great Maryana turkey shoot and then at  Leyte Gulf off the Philippines the  United States Navy will basically  destroy all of the naval air capability  the Japanese Imperial fleet and a series  of submarine attacks and air attacks by  the end of 1944 the Imperial Navy has  rendered combat ineffective now what  does that mean that means while there's  still a few submarines it means that  every time the US Marines and Army  decide to take an island there's not  going to be a Japanese plane appearing  out of the skies to bomb a transport  ship an in Finnish if when Americans  landed  Iwo Jima or Okinawa they're not going to  be straight by Japanese planes and their  only resort will be as we discussed  earlier a desperate attempt to revive a  naval arm or even a narrow arm through  kamikaze but it will mean that the  United States can supply oil munitions  and repair parts anywhere it wants in  the Pacific and it will start - as in  the case of an onion peel away layer  after layer after layer of the Japanese  Empire and it will do so by fighting a  war completely different than what it  fought in Europe and this is really  important to understand the calculus of  world war two we get caught up in this  notion of well we we  70% of our material and our labor to  Europe because Hitler was deemed the  greater threat and then we shorted the  Pacific War and it wasn't that simple as  nobody in the right mind  understood the capabilities of the US  economy at the end of the war in terms  of gross domestic product the United  States is producing more goods and  services than Russia Japan that defunct  Italians the Germans and the British  Empire combined and what that means is  that they are able to conduct two wars  simultaneously through the greater  industrial production of all their  enemies and allies combined and more  importantly they tend to be  complementary rather than antithetical  by that I mean when you build a tank you  you can send some to the Pacific but you  usually send it to Europe when you build  a hundred and five or 155 millimeter  horowitz or gun you send it to Europe if  you have a p-51 or a p-47 or beef 17 you  use it more in Europe same as two of the  b-24 however if you're building  submarines if you're building cruisers  destroyers battleships carriers carrier  craft like the Hellcat fighter or the  courser marine fighter you can direct or  the b-29 heavy bomber you direct all of  that to the Pacific and because Britain  at the same time is fighting in Burma  and trying to protect India and because  of the demise of the Italian and German  fleet Britain is doing the same thing  it's sending its carrier and battleship  fleet to the Pacific and this reaches a  crescendo as I said in earlier 1945  where the Japanese Imperial fleet has  disappeared from the Pacific as large as  it is as formidable as it is it's a it's  a huge vast ocean but by mid 1945 the  American fleet can do what it wants it  can supply it can deploy wherever it  wants and  hope of the Japanese war effort itself  that is the Japanese Imperial fleet the  dream of Admiral Yamamoto is now dashed  and it will be simply a question of  whether the fleet as in the case of the  Air Arm will be so superior to the  Japanese whether it will be it will  preclude a land engagement altogether so  far we've talked about air power and  naval power in terms of redirecting  fighting on the ground up into the air  at sea on the premise that if you were  able to achieve first superiority and  second supremacy then those theaters  could be redirected to the ground that  is you could use ships to attack our  ground forces are to augment or enhance  ground forces or you could do the same  with planes but central that argument is  the ground is where the action is the  ground is where the war will be won or  lost in our next lecture we're going to  see why that is so and why the Allies  were able to defeat Germany and Italy  and Japan on the ground  you

